# One author, seven identities.
#
# The history carries four addresses for alex09x/Alex plus a second GitHub
# account (dip-labs), all the same person. Without this file `git shortlog -sne`
# reports 56 commits split across six identities, which reads as a team that
# never existed - and that reading has to be explained away to a journal editor
# and to anyone auditing authorship.
#
# .mailmap fixes how git *reports* history: git log, git shortlog, git blame. It
# does not rewrite commit objects, and NOTHING else reads it.
#
# In particular it does not reach GitHub, which attributes a commit by the e-mail
# on the object. With this file in place `git shortlog -sne` shows one identity
# while the repository's contributors list still shows three - and GitHub is the
# view an outside reader actually looks at. Measured 2026-09-18; the addresses,
# what each one can be re-attributed with, and what cannot be moved at all are in
# the knowledge base under kyb-authorship-git-vs-github.
#
# Citation metadata is a third, separate thing: Zenodo and GitHub's "Cite this
# repository" box read CITATION.cff. All of it must be settled before a DOI is
# minted, because a Zenodo deposition is an immutable archive.
#
# The canonical address below is a controlled domain rather than a GitHub
# noreply address, which is not a durable citation contact. Changing the
# canonical name or address is a one-line edit here and in CITATION.cff - do it
# before the deposition, not after.
#
# Verify with:  git shortlog -sne HEAD   ->  a single line.

